## Example 2: Switching from DSA to Web Development 1 month before placements
**Month 1-7:** Study DSA intensively. Solve 250 LeetCode problems. Practice system design. Confident for placements.

**Month 8:** Sudden realization: "Web Dev is more fun and has better salary at startups." Abandon DSA prep. Start MERN bootcamp. Learn React, Node.js, databases.

**Month 8.5:** Bootcamp is 8 weeks. Won't finish until Month 10. But placement season is Month 9-10 (campus drives happen in September-October).

**Month 9:** Campus drives active. TCS, Infosys, Amazon all hiring. But you're mid-MERN bootcamp (60% complete). FOMO kicks in. Try to resume DSA in 2 weeks. Can't. DSA knowledge rusty (2 weeks away from practice).

**Month 9.5:** Take Amazon OA: fail DSA round (knowledge decayed, coding reflex lost). Infosys: fail DSA round (same reason). Regret switching.

**Month 10:** Finally finish MERN bootcamp. Build portfolio. But campus drives are almost done. Miss TCS, Infosys, Amazon windows.

**Month 11:** Apply to startups with fresh MERN portfolio. Some interest. But startups also conduct DSA rounds (most do 2-3 DSA problems before MERN). Your DSA is rusty; interviews flop.

**Month 12:** Get local startup offer (₹3.2 LPA) after 20+ rejections. Peers who stayed DSA-focused got TCS/Infosys (₹3.5 LPA) + or Amazon internships.

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Switching from DSA to Web Dev 1 month before campus drives meant abandoning prepared skill for unprepared skill during critical placement window; campus drives were the fastest path to placement.

**Alternate Path:** Finish DSA + placements (Month 8-10). Post-placement or internship, switch to Web Dev (Month 11+). Or: delay switch until after placement.

