## Example 6: Switching specialization in 3rd year (too late for internships)
**Year 1-2:** Study electronics engineering. Do 1-2 internships in electronics.

**Year 3 (Month 1):** Realize "I love coding, not electronics." Switch to CS specialization mid-degree (rare but possible in some colleges). Now starting from zero in CS fundamentals.

**Year 3 (Month 2-8):** Catch up on data structures, algorithms, databases that other CS students learned in Year 1-2. Intensively study. But also need to do year-3 coursework.

**Year 3 (Month 9):** Ready for internships. But CS internship market is competitive; most internships filled by Year 2 students in July. Year 3 spots limited.

**Year 3 (Month 10-11):** Get a less-desirable internship. By this time, placements in the same year are starting. Internship + placement prep conflict.

**Year 4 (Month 1-3):** Placements. You're behind peers: less CS internship experience, rushed fundamental knowledge. Offers mediocre.

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Switching specialization in Year 3 meant cramming 2 years of CS fundamentals in 1 year, plus limited internship opportunities (filled by Year 2 students), and simultaneous placement prep—a recipe for scattered preparation.

**Alternate Path:** Switch in Year 2 (if possible), giving 2 years to learn CS properly and internship experience in Year 2-3.

