## Example 12: Changing fields in 3rd year (from CSE to competitive programming focus)
**Year 1-2:** Standard CS coursework. Average interest. No competitive programming.

**Year 3 (Month 1):** Attend a competitive programming workshop. Inspired. Decide: "I'll become a CP champion and get placed at Google/Microsoft through CP ranks."

**Year 3 (Month 2-6):** Shift focus from regular CS skills to competitive programming. Participate in CodeChef, Codeforces contests weekly. Ranks improving but time-consuming. Regular DSA (interview-style) neglected.

**Year 3 (Month 7-8):** Competitive programming ranks: top 5000 on CodeChef (decent but not Google-level). But interview DSA weak (didn't practice interview-style problems). Time for placement prep.

**Year 3 (Month 9):** Google interview for new grad: asked a system design question. Stammer (never practiced). Asked a DSA problem: slower than expected (competitive programming mindset is different—trick-focused, not optimal-solution focused). Rejected.

**Year 3 (Month 10):** Other FAANG interviews: similar issues. Competitive programming didn't help; in fact, it was detrimental (wasted time on non-interview skills). Get TCS offer (₹3.5 LPA).

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Changing focus to competitive programming in Year 3 (placement-critical year) meant abandoning interview-style DSA at the wrong time; competitive programming ranking is not correlated with interview performance.

**Alternate Path:** Year 1-3: Focus on interview DSA + regular CS skills. Year 4+ (post-placement): pursue competitive programming as hobby.

