## Example 17: Attempting to learn 3 frameworks post-placement offers (trying to be competitive)
**Month 1-8:** DSA focus. Get offers from TCS and Infosys Month 8.

**Month 9:** Realize both offers are backend support roles (₹3.5 LPA). Startups online hiring going on (promise ₹4-5 LPA for full-stack roles). Panic: "Should I learn React, Vue, and Angular to be competitive for startups?"

**Month 9.5:** Start learning React (2 weeks). Simultaneously, TCS tells you to start joining the role (remote work, orientation Month 10). Also, startup interview rounds for 2 startups scheduled for Month 10.

**Month 10:** Learning React 10 hours/week. TCS onboarding 20 hours/week. Startup interviews happening. Interview 1: "Build a React component on the fly." Struggle (learned React only 2 weeks ago). Rejected.

**Month 10.5:** Interview 2 (different startup): "Build a full-stack feature." Back-end knowledge (from DSA) good, but frontend (React) weak. Rejected again.

**Month 11:** Completely committed to TCS now. Stop trying startups. React learning abandoned. Both startups now have different candidates. Missed startup window.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Attempting to learn React post-placement-offer (while joining TCS) meant competing in startup interviews without adequate preparation; should have learned React before receiving TCS offer, or deferred startup applications.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-8: React + DSA (parallel). Better placed for startups during Month 8 offers. Or: Month 8-10: Accept TCS, don't try startups (opportunity cost > payoff).

