## Example 25: Rejecting an offer too early, assuming better will come (timing of negotiation)
**Month 5:** Receive Amazon offer (₹4.8 LPA). Think: "Let me wait for Flipkart/Microsoft offers too."

**Month 5.5:** Amazon: "You have 1 week to accept." Decline to wait. Amazon offer withdrawn.

**Month 6:** Flipkart interview fails at phone screen. Microsoft: doesn't call (resume didn't pass screening). Google: doesn't call either.

**Month 7:** Only TCS remains. Accept offer (₹3.5 LPA). Regret declining Amazon (₹4.8 LPA).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Rejecting an offer without backup offers in hand meant betting on future interviews; but future interviews are uncertain, and rejecting a concrete offer is high-risk.

**Alternate Path:** Accept Amazon (Month 5.5). Don't decline. If Flipkart offers more (Month 6), negotiate release from Amazon. But secure Amazon first.

---

## Example 26: Starting projects too late (after placement offers)
**Month 1-8:** Focus on DSA + system design. No portfolio projects. Get offers from TCS, Infosys, Amazon (Month 8).

**Month 9:** Realize portfolio projects look good. Start building: E-commerce app, Task management app, Chat application. Parallel project building.

**Month 9.5:** Projects in progress. But offers already secured. Less motivation. Projects progress slowly.

**Month 10-11:** Building projects while working (TCS started remote work). Projects 60% complete.

**Month 12:** Post-placement interviews, some startups interested in portfolio projects. But projects are incomplete.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Building portfolio projects after securing placement offers meant less urgency and motivation; projects should be built before or during interview season to showcase skills.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-5: Build 1-2 projects while learning. Month 6-8: Polish projects, use in interviews. Month 9+: New projects post-placement.

---

## Example 27: Switching interview focus in last 2 weeks (from technical to behavioral)
**Month 1-8:** Focus 100% on technical skills: DSA, system design. Zero behavioral interview prep.

**Month 8.5:** First interview feedback: "Your technical is strong, but communication and teamwork seemed weak." Realize: behavioral is half the interview.

**Month 9 (Week 1):** Start behavioral prep. Learn STAR method. Practice 5 behavioral questions. Two weeks to interview.

**Month 9 (Week 2):** Interview: technical solid. Behavioral question: "Tell me about a conflict." Use STAR, but answer feels scripted (learned 2 weeks ago). Interviewer: "Okay, moving on." Sense lack of authenticity.

**Result:** Should have prepared behavioral from Month 1 (naturally internalize through practice). Last-minute prep = surface-level answers.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Ignoring behavioral interview prep for 8 months meant insufficient practice time; behavioral interviews need 4+ weeks of practice to feel natural, not 2 weeks.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-8: Parallel technical (60%) + behavioral (40% via mock interviews). Interview-ready in all areas.

---

## Example 28: Accepting a lower salary immediately, negotiating later (timing of negotiation)
**Month 5:** Receive Amazon offer: ₹4.8 LPA. Think: "Salary okay, I'll negotiate later." Accept immediately.

**Month 6-8:** Work for 2 months (remote work starting). Learn company, team, project. Perform well.

**Month 8:** Ask manager: "Can we revisit salary? I've contributed well." Manager: "Salary finalized at offer. Can't change mid-year. Revisit next review (annual)."

**Month 12:** Annual review: get 10% hike. New salary: ₹5.28 LPA. Regret: if negotiated at offer (Month 5), could have gotten ₹5.2+ LPA immediately.

**Result:** Cost of not negotiating at offer time: ₹4800 (difference between accepting at ₹4.8 and negotiating to ₹5.2).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Accepting offer without negotiating at offer-time (when leverage is high) meant losing negotiation opportunity; once hired, salary is fixed until annual review.

**Alternate Path:** At offer-time (Month 5), negotiate: "Can we improve to ₹5.2 LPA?" Likely yes (candidate is valuable, negotiation expected). Accept at higher salary immediately.

---

## Example 29: Resting too much before final interviews
**Month 1-7:** Intensive prep. Solve 250 DSA problems, 10 system design mocks. Burned out. But ready.

**Month 8 (Week 1):** Final interviews starting (Flipkart, Amazon, Microsoft all scheduled). Plan: rest for 1 week, then give interviews.

**Month 8 (Week 1-2):** Rest. Don't solve problems, don't study, don't mock interview. Just relax.

**Month 8 (Week 2):** Amazon interview (first interview after rest). Expected: refreshed, sharp. Reality: rusty. Took 35 mins to solve a problem usually solved in 20 mins. Barely passed. Interviewer: "Slower than expected."

**Month 8 (Week 3):** Microsoft interview: recovered (did 5 problems before interview). Sharp. Passed decisively.

**Result:** Too much rest (2 weeks) meant skill decay; 3-4 days rest is optimal, but 2+ weeks is harmful.

**Risk Level:** LOW-MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Resting for 2 weeks before final interviews meant skill decay; optimal rest is 3-4 days to refresh mental energy without forgetting muscle memory.

**Alternate Path:** Rest 3-4 days before interview round. Do 5-10 problems on interview day (morning) to warm up. Then interview.

---

## Example 30: Scheduling interviews too close together (no time to improve between failures)
**Month 9 (Week 1, Day 1):** Flipkart phone screen: fail (weak system design). Learn: system design needs more depth.

**Month 9 (Week 1, Day 2):** Amazon phone screen scheduled (next day). No time to learn from Flipkart failure. Make same system design mistakes. Rejected.

**Month 9 (Week 1, Day 3):** Microsoft phone screen (same day). Again, fail due to system design. Rejected.

**Month 9 (Week 2):** Take break. Do 3 system design mocks. Learning: back-of-envelope math, database partitioning, caching strategies.

**Month 9 (Week 3):** Google phone screen: apply learnings from 3 mocks. Solid system design. Pass.

**Result:** Interviews on consecutive days (Day 1-3) didn't allow learning/improvement. After a break and mocks (Day 4-10), improved significantly.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Scheduling interviews too close together meant no time to learn from failures; interviews should be spaced 5-7 days apart to allow feedback incorporation.

**Alternate Path:** Limit to 1 interview per week. After each, analyze feedback, practice 3 mocks, then next interview.

---

## Example 31: Waiting for internship offer until Month 9 to apply for placements
**Month 1-8:** Search for internship casually. Apply to 5-10 companies. No offers.

**Month 9:** Give up on internship. Start intensive placement prep (should have started months ago). Solve 100 DSA problems in 4 weeks (rush).

**Month 9.5:** Campus placement drives active. Unprepared (only 100 problems). Fail TCS, Infosys OAs.

**Result:** Waiting for internship offer (uncertain) delayed placement prep (certain); should have committed to placement prep in Month 1 regardless of internship.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Treating internship as prerequisite for placements meant delaying certain placement prep for uncertain internship outcome; should pursue both in parallel, or prioritize placements.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-4: Casual internship applications. Month 1-8: Steady placement prep. By Month 8, ready for placements regardless of internship status.

---

## Example 32: Taking a break between offer and joining date (too long, skill decay)
**Month 5:** Receive TCS offer. Joining date: Month 8 (3-month gap). Decide to rest and travel.

**Month 5-8:** Vacation, relaxation. No coding. No technical study.

**Month 8:** First day at TCS. Given a coding task. Attempted. Slow. Code quality poor (forgotten concepts). Manager surprised: "You were just interviewed, why is your code below par?" Embarrassing.

**Month 8-9:** Catch up on basics. Takes 1 month to regain skill level. Frustration.

**Result:** 3-month gap with no coding = skill decay. Should have done 1 hour/day coding in the gap.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Waiting 3 months between offer and joining without maintaining skills meant skill decay; muscle memory for coding drops significantly after 4+ weeks of no practice.

**Alternate Path:** Between offer and joining: code 5-10 hours/week (maintain skills). Learn new tools/languages relevant to company. Start work strong.

---

## Example 33: Changing goals in Month 10 (from backend to frontend)
**Month 1-9:** Prep for backend roles. Learn Java, databases, API design. 200 DSA problems. Ready for backend positions.

**Month 10:** Realization: "Frontend is more fun, I want to switch." Start learning React.

**Month 10.5:** Only 2 weeks to interviews. React knowledge superficial. Interview for frontend role at Flipkart: asked to debug a React component. Stammer (barely started React). Rejected.

**Month 11:** Realize switching is mistake. Go back to backend for TCS interviews. Get offer.

**Result:** 2-week React distraction cost Flipkart opportunity. If stayed backend-focused, might have gotten Flipkart backend role (₹4+ LPA).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Changing goals in Month 10 (2 weeks before interviews) meant insufficient time to learn new specialization; specialization switch should happen 8+ weeks before interviews.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-9: Backend-focused. Get backend offers. Post-placement, learn frontend (1-2 months). Switch to frontend-focused roles later.

---

## Example 34: Delaying college group projects, completing them last-minute (during placements)
**Semester 6:** College assigns 3 group projects (semester-long, due Month 10). Low priority initially.

**Month 1-8:** Focus on placements prep. Projects stalled (group members also busy). Deadlines approach.

**Month 9:** Realization: projects due in 3 weeks. Start crunch. Allocate 20 hours/week to projects. Placement interview season also starts. Time conflict.

**Month 9.5:** Complete projects last-minute (quality mediocre). Projects submitted. Same week, Amazon/Flipkart interviews. Mental energy depleted from project crunch. Interviews subpar. Rejected.

**Result:** Last-minute project completion created time conflict with critical interviews. Projects should have been done earlier.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Delaying college projects until Month 9 (interview season) meant simultaneous project crunch + interview prep; projects should be completed by Month 8.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-6: Steady project progress (2-3 hours/week). Projects completed by Month 8. Month 9-11: Interview-focused.

---

## Example 35: Applying to companies only after placements start (missing early hiring)
**Month 1-7:** Passive job search. No active applications. Hoping for campus drives.

**Month 8:** Campus drives not arriving (small college, limited visits). Panic. Start applying to off-campus positions.

**Month 8.5:** Apply to Amazon, Flipkart, Google off-campus. Receive rejection: "Hiring round closed (filled slots)." Already hired from other colleges.

**Month 9:** Apply to startups. Interviews happening. But other candidates applied 1 month earlier (have prepared more). Rejected.

**Result:** Campus drives didn't come. Late off-campus applications missed early hiring windows.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Relying solely on campus drives (passive) meant no backup plan; once campus drives end, hiring windows close, and off-campus applications arrive late.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-7: Light campus prep + off-campus applications (in parallel). Month 8-10: Intensive interviews (both campus + off-campus options open).

---

## Example 36: Starting full-stack preparation too late (both frontend and backend aren't competitive)
**Month 1-6:** DSA focus. 200 problems.

**Month 7:** Realize full-stack roles are trendy. Start learning: Node.js backend (2 weeks), React frontend (2 weeks). Also continue DSA (1 week).

**Month 7.5:** Backend: basic REST API. Frontend: basic React. DSA: 250 problems total. But none are deep.

**Month 8:** Interview at startup (full-stack role): asked to build a feature. Backend: schema design weak (didn't practice database design). Frontend: React knowledge superficial. DSA strong (but less relevant for startup full-stack). Rejected: "You're strong in DSA but weak in full-stack."

**Month 9:** Interview at Amazon (backend role): strong DSA. But interviewer: "Your backend design looks hurried (learned it in 2 weeks, shows)." Rejected at advanced round.

**Result:** Trying full-stack (both frontend + backend) in 4 weeks meant both were weak. Should have chosen one specialization.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Switching to full-stack only 1 month before interviews (Month 7) meant insufficient depth in both areas; full-stack requires 8+ weeks (4 weeks backend, 4 weeks frontend, with projects).

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-7: DSA + decide on specialization (backend or frontend). Month 8-12: Specialize + build projects. Post-placement, learn the other.

---

## Example 37: Not starting CGPA repair until Month 8 (too late, irreversible)
**Semester 1-5 (Month 1-7):** Casual effort on studies. CGPA drops to 7.0. Realize in Month 8 this is below cutoff (7.5).

**Month 8:** Talk to professor: "Can I retake courses to improve CGPA?" Answer: "No, retakes apply to future semesters' CGPA calculation, not cumulative." Current CGPA: 7.0 (locked in).

**Month 8.5:** Ineligible for TCS (7.5+ required). Ineligible for Infosys (7.5+ required). Ineligible for Amazon (7.5+ required). Only HCL (no cutoff).

**Result:** CGPA locked in Month 8 (too late to change). Should have maintained 7.5+ from Semester 1.

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Allowing CGPA to drop to 7.0 without repair attempt until Month 8 meant irreversible damage; CGPA cutoffs are hard, and low semester GPA can't be retroactively improved.

**Alternate Path:** Maintain 7.5+ GPA per semester from Semester 1. Even if one semester is 7.3, other semesters at 7.7+ can balance. CGPA accumulated, can't be erased.

---

## Example 38: Negotiating salary after accepting offer (weak position)
**Month 5:** Receive ₹4.5 LPA offer. Accept immediately (happy with offer).

**Month 6:** Friend mentions: "I got ₹4.8 LPA from same company." Realize you negotiated. Email HR: "Can we revise offer to ₹4.8 LPA?"

**Response:** "You already accepted at ₹4.5 LPA. We can't change now. Terms finalized." Stuck at ₹4.5 LPA.

**Result:** Negotiating after acceptance has zero leverage. Should have negotiated before accepting.

**Risk Level:** LOW-MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Accepting offer before negotiating meant lost leverage; negotiation window exists only at offer-time, not after acceptance.

**Alternate Path:** At offer-time (before acceptance), ask: "Is there room to increase to ₹4.8 LPA given X, Y, Z (reasons)?" Likely approved. Then accept.

---

## Example 39: Starting mock interviews too close to real interviews
**Month 1-8:** Self-study DSA, system design videos. No mock interviews.

**Month 8.5:** Start mock interviews. Schedule 5 mocks in next 2 weeks. First mock: many mistakes (interview format unfamiliar). Learn from first mock.

**Month 8.7:** Second mock: slight improvement. But real Amazon interview in 3 days. Not enough mock practice to embed learnings.

**Month 9 (Day 1):** Amazon interview: make mistakes from first-second mocks (haven't had enough practice to break habits). Rejected.

**Month 9 (Day 3):** Flipkart interview: incorporated learnings from failed Amazon interview. Better. Passed.

**Result:** First real interview (Amazon) was too early (should have done 8-10 mocks first, not 2). Second real interview (Flipkart) was prepared.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Starting mock interviews only 2 weeks before real interviews meant insufficient mock reps to embed learnings; mock interviews should start 8+ weeks before to allow practice-feedback-improvement cycles.

**Alternate Path:** Month 5-7: Start mocks (2 per week, 8 total). Learn interview patterns. Month 8-9: Real interviews. Better prepared.

---

## Example 40: Finishing DSA prep but neglecting system design until too late
**Month 1-7:** DSA intensive. 250 LeetCode problems. Solid.

**Month 8:** Week 1: Realize system design interview starting Week 2. Start crash course.

**Month 8 (Week 1-2):** Watch system design YouTube videos (15+ hours). No actual mock interviews.

**Month 8.5:** System design interview. Explain approach (video knowledge). Interviewer: "Walk me through the database schema." Detailed questions. Stammer (haven't thought deeply, videos are high-level).

**Result:** Learning system design only 1 week before interview meant surface knowledge. Should have started 6-8 weeks earlier.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Finishing DSA without starting system design meant late start on interactive skill; system design requires mock interviews (4-6 weeks minimum) to develop depth, and videos alone insufficient.

**Alternate Path:** Month 5: Start system design mocks (after DSA 150 problems done). Monthly: 2 mocks/week + DSA. System design ready by Month 8.

---

## Example 41: Changing specialization 1 month before interviews
**Month 1-8:** Prepare for data science roles. Learn Python, ML libraries, statistics. Build ML projects.

**Month 9:** Realize ML job market in India is poor. Data science roles want 2+ years experience. Switch to software engineering (core CS) roles.

**Month 9.5:** Start learning backend (Node.js) + system design. Only 1 month.

**Month 10:** Interview for software engineer role. Asked about system design (weak, only 1 month learned). Asked about backend best practices (weak). Rejected.

**Result:** Switching specialization 1 month before interviews meant insufficient time to learn new domain. Should have switched earlier.

**Risk Level:** HIGH

**Root Cause:** Changing specialization in Month 9 (1 month before interviews) meant learning new domain right at interview time; specialization switch should happen 3-4 months before to allow proper learning.

**Alternate Path:** Month 1-5: Explore both (part-time). Month 6: Commit to one (backend). Month 7-10: Intensive prep.

