## Example 44: Taking college courses in Month 8 (during placements)
**Semester 6:** College decides to add "Elective courses" during Month 8-10 (normally courses in Month 1-7). Last-minute decision.

**Month 8:** Month 8 is also peak placement season. Courses start: Database Systems (lecture 2 hours/week), AI (lecture 2 hours/week). Mandatory attendance.

**Month 8-10:** Course lectures + placement interviews conflict. Attend lectures (mandatory, 40% of grade = attendance). Interviews suffer (mental energy divided).

**Month 9:** Interview at Amazon: weak system design (didn't have time to prepare due to course lectures). Rejected.

**Month 10:** Similar story: Flipkart rejected, TCS accepted (interview wasn't critical enough, less demanding).

**Result:** Surprise college courses in Month 8-10 (peak placement season) meant divided focus.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** College scheduling courses during peak placement season (Month 8-10) created timing conflict; courses should end by Month 7 to leave placements-only months.

**Alternate Path:** Advocate with college to end courses by Month 7. Or: take courses in lighter semester (Month 11-12, post-placements).

