## Example 2: Starting competitive programming because all friends are doing it
**Month 1-3 (Year 2):** All 7 friends join a competitive programming community. They talk daily: "CodeChef rounds this weekend!" "I just got AC on DP problem!" Excitement contagious.

**You:** Don't have natural interest in CP (prefer building projects). But friends' enthusiasm pressures you. Sign up for CodeChef.

**Month 4-8:** Attend weekly CodeChef contests (2-3 hours each). Friends ranking: top 10,000. You ranking: top 50,000 (struggling). Every week: "Why is your rank lower?" Embarrassment. Friends discussing problems; you're lost.

**Month 9:** Realize CP isn't for you (love building projects, not solving puzzles). Want to switch to project-based learning. Friends: "CP is better for placements, why are you quitting?" Pressure to stay.

**Month 10:** Compromise: do both CP (to stay with friends) + projects (your passion). Result: mediocre at both. Time spread thin.

**Year 2 (End):** Neither CP ranking nor projects portfolio impressive. Placements: mediocre job (neither CP nor projects excelled).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Starting CP due to peer pressure (to fit in with friends) instead of intrinsic interest meant wasted time on misaligned activity; placements reward deep expertise, not scattered efforts.

**Alternate Path:** Pursue projects (your passion). Friends do CP. Both activities valid. Meet friends outside of CP (hostels, meals, clubs).

