## Example 3: Taking difficult courses because friends take them
**Month 1 (Semester 5):** Course selection. 6 courses to choose from: 3 hard, 3 easy. Friends all choose 2 hard courses (Algorithms, Operating Systems, Compiler Design). Say: "Okay, I'll choose same."

**Month 2-4:** Attend all lectures. Algorithms requires deep mathematical thinking (you prefer practical coding). Operating Systems requires systems knowledge (you prefer web development). Compiler Design: very theoretical. Struggling in all three.

**Month 5:** Midterms. Algorithms: 55%. OS: 58%. Compiler: 52%. Friends' scores: 78%, 80%, 75% respectively. Huge gap. Friends: "The exams were easy, why are you struggling?" Feel stupid.

**Month 6-7:** Drop one course (Compiler Design, take incomplete grade). Survive Algorithms and OS with C grades (below friends' B+ grades). CGPA drops.

**Month 8:** Realize: hard courses don't suit learning style. Should have taken easy courses + did projects instead. Too late.

**Result:** Hard courses + weak performance = lower CGPA + weaker foundation. Placements affected.

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Choosing difficult courses to match friends' choices instead of picking courses suited to learning style meant poor performance and CGPA damage.

**Alternate Path:** Choose 1-2 hard courses (per genuine interest), 1 medium, 1-2 easy. Balance. Friends choose differently: okay.

