## Example 5: Learning the same specialization as friends (Web Dev) when preference is ML
**Month 6 (Year 2):** Friend group discussion: "Let's all learn Web Dev (MERN stack). Lots of internship opportunities." 4 friends excited. You're interested in ML (neural networks, computer vision). But friends: "Web Dev has better job market, why are you wasting time on ML?"

**Month 7-12 (Year 2):** Learn MERN stack (React, Node.js, MongoDB) to match friends. Build portfolio projects. But heart isn't in it. Building websites feels boring to you (preference for data science).

**Year 3 (Internship search):** Friends get Web Dev internship offers (Unacademy, Razorpay, etc.). You get 1 offer. Turns out to be backend role (Node.js), not ML. Friends: "At least you got an internship, same stack!"

**Year 3 (Internship):** 2-month internship in Web Dev (Node.js backend). Efficient work (you learned MERN). But miserable: not excited about work. Friends in same internships: excited, learning fast.

**Year 3 (End of Internship):** Realize you wasted 6 months learning Web Dev (not your passion). Placements: given offers in Web Dev roles (₹3.8 LPA). But want to work in ML (₹4.5+ LPA for ML engineer at same company).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM-HIGH

**Root Cause:** Learning Web Dev due to peer pressure (friends' enthusiasm, job market argument) instead of pursuing passion (ML) meant misaligned learning; passion + proficiency > proficiency without passion.

**Alternate Path:** Learn ML (your passion). Friends do Web Dev. Both are valid. In placements, ML engineer offers are competitive. Follow interest, not peers.

