## Example 7: Choosing hostel room with friends over better hostel building
**Year 1 (Hostel allotment):** 4 friends assigned to Building A (older, poor internet, far from campus). You assigned to Building B (new, great internet, close to campus). Friends: "Let's all request to be together in Building A!"

**You:** Prefer Building B (better for studies, internet crucial for placements prep). But friends: "We want to be together, don't abandon us." Guilt. Request hostel authority to move to Building A.

**Year 1-3:** In Building A with friends. Fun social life. But poor internet: download speeds 1 Mbps (should be 10+). Watching DSA tutorials = buffering. Online coding: lag. Placement prep hindered by internet.

**Year 3 (Placements):** Video interview with company: lag causes audio cuts. Interviewer frustrated. Didn't perform well (not due to skill, but technical issues). Rejected.

**Result:** Choosing to live with friends (over better infrastructure) meant technical disadvantages during critical placements. ₹50,000 salary impact (worse interview performance).

**Risk Level:** MEDIUM

**Root Cause:** Prioritizing friendship (hostel room together) over individual advantage (better internet for placements) meant compromised interview experience.

**Alternate Path:** Choose Building B. Meet friends in common areas, hostels, classes. Friendship isn't location-dependent. Infrastructure > friendship proximity.

